-- Q1514: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only for publicly traded entities subject to SEC oversight","Only when there is a suspected risk of fraud","Only when the auditor intends to rely on internal controls","For every audit engagement, whether or not controls will be tested"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): AU-C 315 requires the auditor to obtain an understanding of internal control relevant to the audit for every engagement, regardless of whether the auditor plans to test controls. This understanding helps identify potential misstatements and design further audit procedures.
Wrong (A): The requirement applies to all audits, not just publicly traded companies. Private entities are equally subject to AU-C 315.
Wrong (B): Understanding internal control is required on every engagement, not only when fraud is suspected.
Wrong (C): Even if the auditor does not plan to rely on controls, the understanding is still required to assess risks of material misstatement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1514;

-- Q1516: Risk Assessment [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reviewing accounting estimates for biases indicating fraud","Observing the entity''s physical inventory count process","Confirming the balances of accounts payable with vendors","Performing a detailed search for any unrecorded liabilities"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): AU-C 240 requires three specific procedures to address management override risk: testing journal entries, reviewing accounting estimates for biases that could result in material misstatement due to fraud, and evaluating the business rationale for significant unusual transactions.
Wrong (B): Observing inventory counts addresses the existence assertion for inventory but is not a required procedure specifically targeting management override risk.
Wrong (C): Confirming accounts payable addresses completeness and existence of payables, not management override of controls.
Wrong (D): Searching for unrecorded liabilities is a substantive procedure for completeness, not one of the three required management override procedures under AU-C 240.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1516;

-- Q1517: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether the entity''s main competitors are publicly traded","Only current year transactions and account balances","Only the entity''s historical financial performance trends","Impact on asset values, going concern, and obsolescence risk"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315.A37-A39, inherent risk factors include the nature of the entity''s industry and economic conditions. Rapid technological change and declining demand increase the risk of inventory obsolescence, asset impairment, and going concern doubt across multiple assertions.
Wrong (A): The public trading status of competitors is not a relevant inherent risk factor for the entity''s financial statement assertions.
Wrong (B): Inherent risk assessment requires consideration of industry and economic conditions beyond just current year transactions.
Wrong (C): Focusing only on historical performance ignores forward-looking factors like technological disruption and declining demand that affect current asset valuations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1517;

-- Q1518: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["IT general controls, application controls, and interface risks","Only general controls over the primary financial reporting system","Whether the entity has adopted any cloud-based platforms","Only those controls previously tested by the IT audit team"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 315.A54-A56, the auditor should consider IT general controls, application controls, and the risk that interfaces between multiple integrated systems may introduce errors through data translation or timing issues.
Wrong (B): Focusing only on the primary reporting system ignores risks from integrated subsidiary systems and the interfaces between them.
Wrong (C): Whether the entity uses cloud-based systems is a narrow consideration that does not encompass the full scope of IT risk assessment required.
Wrong (D): The auditor cannot simply rely on work performed by the entity''s own IT audit team without independent evaluation of control design and effectiveness.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1518;

-- Q1519: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Communicate the identified risk directly to the entity''s legal counsel","Issue a modified audit report due to the significant risk identified","Design further audit procedures responsive to the assessed risks","Always perform both tests of controls and substantive procedures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 330, when the auditor identifies risks at the assertion level, the auditor must design and perform further audit procedures whose nature, timing, and extent are responsive to those assessed risks. The auditor has flexibility to use a combined or substantive-only approach.
Wrong (A): Communicating with legal counsel is not the required response to an assessed risk of misstatement; the auditor must design responsive audit procedures.
Wrong (B): A modified report addresses misstatements found, not risks identified during planning. Risk assessment drives audit procedures, not report modifications.
Wrong (D): The auditor is not required to perform both types of procedures; a substantive-only approach may be appropriate depending on the circumstances.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1519;

-- Q1520: Risk Assessment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Valuation of revenue and cost estimates on long-term contracts","Presentation and disclosure of the entity''s fixed asset balances","Completeness of cash disbursements recorded during the period","Existence of office equipment listed on the asset schedule"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): The percentage-of-completion method requires significant estimates of total contract revenue and costs, which directly affect revenue recognition timing and amounts. Under AU-C 540, these estimates involve substantial judgment and are inherently susceptible to misstatement.
Wrong (B): Presentation and disclosure of fixed assets involves less estimation uncertainty than revenue recognition under percentage-of-completion.
Wrong (C): Cash disbursement completeness is a routine assertion that does not involve the complex estimation judgments inherent in long-term contract accounting.
Wrong (D): Existence of office equipment is a straightforward assertion typically verified through physical inspection, not subject to high estimation risk.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1520;

-- Q1523: Risk Assessment [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Perform no substantive procedures since control risk is assessed low","Test controls to confirm low control risk, then do targeted substantive work","Perform only analytical procedures without any detailed transaction testing","Set detection risk at high because the overall audit risk is moderate"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): When inherent risk is high but control risk is assessed as low, the auditor should perform tests of controls to confirm that controls are operating effectively and then design reduced but targeted substantive procedures. Under AU-C 330, substantive procedures cannot be eliminated entirely even when controls are effective.
Wrong (A): AU-C 330.06 requires substantive procedures for all relevant assertions regardless of the assessed level of control risk; eliminating them entirely is not permitted.
Wrong (C): Analytical procedures alone are insufficient when inherent risk is high; detailed testing of specific transactions is needed to address the assessed risk.
Wrong (D): Setting detection risk at high when inherent risk is high would increase the overall risk of failing to detect material misstatements to an unacceptable level.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1523;

-- Q1525: Risk Assessment [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Address all significant risks with equal procedures regardless of likelihood","Develop a tailored response for each risk based on its specific nature","Focus audit resources only on the single largest identified significant risk","Defer all significant risks to be addressed in the subsequent year''s audit"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 330, each significant risk requires a tailored audit response based on its specific characteristics, including the nature of the risk, the assertions affected, and the likely sources of misstatement. A uniform approach would not effectively address the unique characteristics of each risk.
Wrong (A): Treating all significant risks identically ignores differences in their nature, likelihood, and magnitude, which would result in an ineffective and inefficient audit response.
Wrong (C): Focusing only on the largest risk ignores other significant risks that could individually or collectively lead to material misstatement.
Wrong (D): Deferring significant risks violates the auditor''s responsibility to respond to identified risks in the current period audit under AU-C 330.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1525;

-- Q1526: Risk Assessment [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only opportunity due to the weak governance oversight structure","Detection risk combined with the assessed level of control risk","Only rationalization as the primary fraud triangle condition present","Incentive from compensation structure and opportunity from weak governance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 240, the CEO''s compensation tied to earnings targets creates incentive/pressure to manipulate earnings, while the dual role as CEO and chairman represents weak governance oversight, providing opportunity. This combination of two fraud triangle conditions significantly increases fraud risk.
Wrong (A): Opportunity alone is incomplete because the earnings-based compensation creates a clear incentive/pressure element that must also be recognized.
Wrong (B): Detection risk and control risk are audit risk model components, not fraud triangle conditions. The fraud triangle consists of incentive, opportunity, and rationalization.
Wrong (C): Rationalization alone does not capture the incentive from the compensation structure or the opportunity from the governance weakness clearly present in this scenario.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1526;

-- Q1527: Risk Assessment [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Straight-line depreciation of office furniture with a known useful life","Fair value of Level 3 instruments requiring significant unobservable inputs","Accrued utility expenses estimated from the prior month''s billing cycle","Prepaid insurance calculated directly from the stated insurance policy terms"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 540, Level 3 financial instruments have the highest estimation uncertainty because their fair value relies on significant unobservable inputs and complex valuation models involving substantial judgment and subjectivity, often classified as significant risks.
Wrong (A): Straight-line depreciation with a known useful life involves minimal estimation uncertainty because the inputs are objective and well-defined.
Wrong (C): Accrued utility expenses based on prior billing involve low estimation uncertainty since historical billing provides a reliable basis for the estimate.
Wrong (D): Prepaid insurance based on policy terms requires little judgment because the amounts and periods are contractually specified.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1527;

-- Q1529: Risk Assessment [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["IT risks as low because legacy systems are inherently more stable","High risk of ineffective IT general controls requiring substantive approach","IT risks as irrelevant to the financial statement audit objectives","Only risks related to the newest IT systems in the environment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Outdated security patches, shared administrator passwords, and no change management process indicate significant IT general control weaknesses under AU-C 315.A54-A56. These deficiencies could undermine automated controls and data integrity, potentially requiring a substantive-only audit approach.
Wrong (A): Legacy systems with outdated patches and weak access controls are not inherently more reliable; these characteristics indicate elevated risk of unauthorized changes and data compromise.
Wrong (C): IT risks are directly relevant to the financial statement audit because they affect the reliability of electronically processed transactions and automated controls.
Wrong (D): Assessing only the newest systems ignores the significant control weaknesses in legacy systems that process financial data and interact with other applications.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1529;

-- Q1530: Risk Assessment [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the differences as management''s prerogative without further inquiry","Evaluate whether differences indicate aggressive accounting or bias risk","Change the entity''s accounting policies to align with industry norms","Issue a qualified opinion based solely on the accounting policy differences"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315, significant departures from industry-standard accounting policies may indicate aggressive accounting, management bias, or manipulation of financial results. The auditor should evaluate whether the policies are within the acceptable range under the applicable framework and whether they increase misstatement risk.
Wrong (A): Simply accepting policy differences without inquiry fails to meet the auditor''s obligation to evaluate whether those differences heighten misstatement risk.
Wrong (C): The auditor has no authority to change the entity''s accounting policies; the auditor''s role is to evaluate their appropriateness and effect on audit risk.
Wrong (D): Policy differences alone do not warrant a qualified opinion unless they result in a departure from the applicable financial reporting framework.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1530;

-- Q1531: Risk Assessment [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Rely on the prior year''s tests since the control objectives remain the same","Reassess control design, perform new walkthroughs, and test IT general controls","Skip testing automated controls and use only substantive audit procedures","Accept the ERP vendor''s assurance that all system controls are effective"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315, a new ERP system fundamentally changes the control environment even if control objectives remain the same. The auditor must reassess control design and implementation, perform new walkthroughs, and evaluate IT general controls over the new system before relying on automated controls.
Wrong (A): Prior year test results are not relevant when the underlying system has been replaced; the controls are now implemented differently in the new environment.
Wrong (C): Skipping automated control testing may be unnecessarily costly and ignores the potential to rely on well-designed controls in the new system after proper evaluation.
Wrong (D): Vendor assurances do not constitute sufficient audit evidence; the auditor must independently evaluate control design and operating effectiveness.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1531;

-- Q1535: Internal Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The same employee records all transactions and approves vendor payments","The same employee handles every aspect of the biweekly payroll process","The employee receiving cash also prepares the monthly bank reconciliation","Different employees handle authorization, recording, and asset custody"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315.A78, proper segregation of duties separates the functions of authorization, recording, and custody among different employees. This separation reduces the risk that errors or fraud will occur and go undetected.
Wrong (A): Having the same employee record and approve transactions combines recording and authorization functions, violating segregation of duties principles.
Wrong (B): One employee handling all payroll aspects combines authorization, recording, and custody, creating a high risk of undetected errors or fraud.
Wrong (C): The employee receiving cash (custody) should not also prepare bank reconciliations (a form of recording/verification), as this undermines the control''s detective capability.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1535;

-- Q1537: Internal Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Approving transactions before they are processed by operating staff","Physically safeguarding the organization''s assets from theft or loss","Setting the ethical tone at the top of the entire organization","Ongoing and separate evaluations of whether controls are functioning"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 315.A91, the monitoring component of COSO involves ongoing evaluations, separate evaluations, or a combination of both to ascertain whether the five components of internal control are present and functioning over time.
Wrong (A): Approving transactions before processing is a control activity (specifically an authorization control), not a monitoring activity.
Wrong (B): Physically safeguarding assets is a control activity related to the custody of assets, not part of the monitoring component.
Wrong (C): Setting the ethical tone at the top is a key element of the control environment component, not the monitoring component of the COSO framework.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1537;

-- Q1540: Internal Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Communication flows only downward from top management to staff","Only financial information is communicated to employees and outsiders","Relevant information is captured and communicated in a timely manner","All internal communications must be formally documented and retained"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 315.A94, the information and communication component of COSO requires the entity to identify, capture, and communicate relevant information in a form and timeframe that enables people to carry out their internal control responsibilities.
Wrong (A): Effective communication flows in all directions--downward, upward, and across the organization--not only from management to staff (AU-C 315.A95).
Wrong (B): The component encompasses all relevant information including operational and compliance data, not only financial information.
Wrong (D): While documentation supports good communication, the COSO framework does not require that all internal communications be formally documented and retained.$EXPL$
